Another issue is that when there are several custom messages, and multiple rules can be invalid at the same time, it gets very verbose to both:
1. Suppress the default message
2. Prevent multiple custom messages from showing up

```jsx
<Field.Control
  required
  defaultValue=""
  placeholder="e.g. 4242424242424242"
  className={styles.Input}
  pattern="[0-9\-\\s]+"
  minLength="14"
/>
<Field.Validity>
  {(validity) => {
    const states = Object.entries(validity.validity)
      .map((entry) => (entry[1] ? entry[0] : null))
      .filter((v) => v);

    if (states.includes("patternMismatch")) {
      return (
        <Field.Error className={styles.Error} match="patternMismatch">
          Numbers, spaces and dashes only
        </Field.Error>
      );
    }

    if (states.includes("tooShort")) {
      return (
        <Field.Error className={styles.Error} match="tooShort">
          Too short
        </Field.Error>
      );
    }

    return <Field.Error className={styles.Error} />;
  }}
</Field.Validity>
```

I think there should be more documentation revolved around this.
A lot of validation can be server side, and I'm not sure if this is optimal.

Is there a better more "Based UI" way to implement the following?

```tsx
       <Field
          variant={variant}
          size={sizeVariant}
          name="validity-field"
          style={dynamicStyle}
          validate={(value: unknown) => {
            const lowerValue = (value as string)?.toLowerCase()
            if (lowerValue === 'foo') {
              return 'Invalid input: foo is not allowed'
            }
            return null
          }}
        >
          <Field.Label>Validity Demo</Field.Label>
          <Field.Control
            type="text"
            placeholder="Type 'foo' (invalid) or 'bar' (valid)"
          />
          <Field.Validity>
            {(state) => {
              const inputValue = (state.value as string)?.toLowerCase()

              if (inputValue === 'foo') {
                return <span>✗ Invalid input</span>
              } else if (inputValue === 'bar') {
                return <span>✓ Valid input</span>
              } else {
                return null
              }
            }}
          </Field.Validity>
        </Field>
```
